<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch6-page3-activities" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>More repeated game examples</title>

  <p>
    On this page, we'll get more practice analyzing repeated games. These
    examples are not in the book. There is no new theory or definitions
    here, but the practice should help before you tackle the homework
    questions! The examples are labeled <q>activities</q>, since you'll be
    working through each part yourself.
  </p>

  <p>
    We'll look at repeated versions of a classic game, Chicken. First,
    here is the table for a single game of Chicken. Each teenager either goes <term>straight</term>
    or <term>swerves</term>, with payoffs listed as (Teenager 1, Teenager 2).
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-chicken-stage">
    <title>Chicken</title>
    <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Teenager 2: straight</cell>
        <cell>Teenager 2: swerve</cell>
      </row>
      <row bottom="minor">
        <cell>Teenager 1: straight</cell>
        <cell><m>(-2, -2)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(1, -1)</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Teenager 1: swerve</cell>
        <cell><m>(-1, 1)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Unlike the Prisoner's Dilemma, Chicken has two pure strategy Nash
    equilibria: (straight, swerve) and (swerve, straight). In each one, one
    teenager goes straight and the other swerves.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Activity 1: a Nash equilibrium of repeated Chicken           -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <p>
    In the next two activities, we'll find a Nash equilibrium of repeated
    Chicken, show why it is one, and then check whether it is subgame
    perfect. This is the same process we used for the trigger strategy on
    the previous page.
  </p>

  <activity xml:id="activity-chicken-nash">
    <title>Swerving, Backed by a Threat</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The two teenagers play Chicken over and over, in rounds
        <m>k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots</m>, with discount factor <m>\delta</m>, where
        <m>0 \lt \delta \lt 1</m>. Consider the following strategy, which we'll call <m>\gamma</m>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Swerve as long as both teenagers have swerved in every earlier
            round.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <em>either</em> teenager has ever gone straight, go straight
            in every round from then on.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is Chicken's version of the trigger strategy: the threat is
        <q>if anyone goes straight, we crash every round from now on.</q>
        We'll show that <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium when
        <m>\delta \geq \frac{1}{2}</m>, following the same steps as the
        proof of Theorem 6.1. Suppose Teenager 1 uses <m>\gamma</m>, and
        we'll check whether Teenager 2 can do better by switching to a
        different strategy.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="activity-chicken-nash-both">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose both teenagers use <m>\gamma</m>. Fill in the table below,
          and find <m>\pi_2(\gamma, \gamma)</m>.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell>round 1</cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Teenager 1's move</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Teenager 2's move</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Teenager 2's payoff</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Nobody ever goes straight first, so both swerve in every round:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell>round 1</cell><cell>round 2</cell><cell>round 3</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Teenager 1's move</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Teenager 2's move</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Teenager 2's payoff</cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Teenager 2 gets 0 every round, so
          <m>\pi_2(\gamma, \gamma) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="activity-chicken-nash-never">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose Teenager 1 keeps using <m>\gamma</m>, but Teenager 2
          switches to some other strategy, <m>\gamma'</m>. First, suppose
          <m>\gamma'</m> never goes straight. What happens in every round?
          What is <m>\pi_2(\gamma, \gamma')</m>? Does Teenager 2 do any better
          than with <m>\gamma</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Teenager 2 swerves in every round, so nobody ever goes straight, and
          <m>\gamma</m> tells Teenager 1 to swerve in every round too. Both
          swerve forever, just as with <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m>, so
          <m>\pi_2(\gamma, \gamma') = 0 = \pi_2(\gamma, \gamma)</m>. Teenager 2
          does no better.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="activity-chicken-nash-table">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Otherwise, <m>\gamma'</m> goes straight at some point. Say Teenager
          2 goes straight for the first time in round <m>k</m>. Then:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              In rounds 0 through <m>k-1</m>, nobody has gone straight, so
              both teenagers swerve.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              In round <m>k</m>, Teenager 1 still swerves, since nobody had
              gone straight before round <m>k</m>, and Teenager 2 goes
              straight.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Fill in the rest of the table, assuming that from round
          <m>k+1</m> on, Teenager 2 makes their best move in every round.
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell>round <m>k-1</m></cell><cell>round <m>k</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+1</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+2</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Teenager 1's move (<m>\gamma</m>)</cell><cell colspan="3">swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Teenager 2's move (<m>\gamma'</m>)</cell><cell colspan="3">swerve</cell><cell>straight</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Teenager 2's discounted payoff</cell><cell colspan="3"><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\delta^k</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          From round <m>k+1</m> on, Teenager 2 has gone straight, so
          <m>\gamma</m> tells Teenager 1 to go straight in every round, no
          matter what Teenager 2 does. What is Teenager 2's best response to a
          teenager who goes straight?
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From round <m>k+1</m> on, Teenager 1 goes straight in every round.
          Against that, Teenager 2 gets <m>-1</m> by swerving and <m>-2</m> by
          going straight, and nothing Teenager 2 does can change Teenager 1's
          later moves. So Teenager 2's best move is to swerve in every round,
          for <m>-1</m> each round:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell><cell>round 0</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell><cell>round <m>k-1</m></cell><cell>round <m>k</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+1</m></cell><cell>round <m>k+2</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Teenager 1's move (<m>\gamma</m>)</cell><cell colspan="3">swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell>straight</cell><cell>straight</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Teenager 2's move (<m>\gamma'</m>)</cell><cell colspan="3">swerve</cell><cell>straight</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell>swerve</cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Teenager 2's discounted payoff</cell><cell colspan="3"><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>\delta^k</m></cell><cell><m>-\delta^{k+1}</m></cell><cell><m>-\delta^{k+2}</m></cell><cell><m>\cdots</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          This is different from the Wine Merchant game. There, after Bernard
          defected, his best move was to <em>keep</em> defecting. Here, after
          Teenager 2 goes straight once, their best move is to go back to
          swerving.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="activity-chicken-nash-sum">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the tables, here are Teenager 2's payoffs for the two
          strategies:
          <md>
            <mrow>\pi_2(\gamma, \gamma) \amp = \underbrace{0 + 0 + \cdots + 0}_{\text{rounds } 0 \text{ to } k-1} + 0 + 0 + 0 + \cdots</mrow>
            <mrow>\pi_2(\gamma, \gamma') \amp = \underbrace{0 + 0 + \cdots + 0}_{\text{rounds } 0 \text{ to } k-1} + \delta^k - \delta^{k+1} - \delta^{k+2} - \cdots</mrow>
          </md>
          The terms for rounds 0 through <m>k-1</m> are the same, so we are
          really comparing
          <me>0 + 0 + 0 + \cdots = 0 \qquad \text{with} \qquad \delta^k - \delta^{k+1} - \delta^{k+2} - \cdots.</me>
          Find the sum <m>\delta^k - \delta^{k+1} - \delta^{k+2} - \cdots</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          After the first term, the rest is a geometric series:
          <m>-\delta^{k+1} - \delta^{k+2} - \cdots</m> has
          <m>a = -\delta^{k+1}</m> and <m>r = \delta</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The only positive term is the first one, from round <m>k</m>.
          Everything after it is a geometric series:
          <me>\delta^k - \delta^{k+1} - \delta^{k+2} - \cdots = \underbrace{\delta^k}_{\text{round } k} + \underbrace{\left(-\delta^{k+1} - \delta^{k+2} - \cdots\right)}_{a = -\delta^{k+1},\ r = \delta}.</me>
          So
          <me>\delta^k - \delta^{k+1} - \delta^{k+2} - \cdots = \delta^k - \frac{\delta^{k+1}}{1 - \delta}.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="activity-chicken-nash-compare">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Going straight in round <m>k</m> does <em>not</em> help Teenager 2
          when <m>\pi_2(\gamma, \gamma') \leq \pi_2(\gamma, \gamma)</m>, that
          is, when
          <me>\delta^k - \frac{\delta^{k+1}}{1 - \delta} \leq 0.</me>
          For which values of <m>\delta</m> is this true? Conclude that
          <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> is a Nash equilibrium for those values of
          <m>\delta</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Divide both sides by <m>\delta^k</m>, just as in the proof of
          Theorem 6.1.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Dividing by <m>\delta^k</m> gives
          <m>1 - \frac{\delta}{1 - \delta} \leq 0</m>. Multiplying by
          <m>1 - \delta</m>, which is positive, gives
          <m>1 - \delta - \delta \leq 0</m>, so <m>\delta \geq \frac{1}{2}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          So when <m>\delta \geq \frac{1}{2}</m>, no strategy <m>\gamma'</m>
          gives Teenager 2 more than <m>\pi_2(\gamma, \gamma)</m>: if
          <m>\gamma'</m> never goes straight, Teenager 2 gets the same payoff,
          and if it first goes straight in round <m>k</m>, the best Teenager 2
          can do is the payoff we just found, which is at most 0. So
          <m>\gamma</m> is a best response to <m>\gamma</m>. By symmetry, the
          same is true for Teenager 1, so <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> is a Nash
          equilibrium when <m>\delta \geq \frac{1}{2}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p>
    The strategy <m>\gamma</m> contains a threat. Each teenager starts out
    cooperating, by swerving, but threatens that if anyone ever goes
    straight, they'll go straight forever, so the teenagers crash every
    round. If <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> is a subgame perfect Nash
    equilibrium, then the threat is <em>believable</em>: carrying it out
    is a best response in every subgame, including the subgames after
    someone has gone straight, where the threat actually has to be carried
    out. If it isn't subgame perfect, then the threat only works because it
    is never tested.
  </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <!-- Activity 2: is it subgame perfect?                            -->
  <!-- ============================================================ -->

  <activity xml:id="activity-chicken-spne">
    <title>Is the Threat Believable?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now let's check whether <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> is a subgame
        perfect Nash equilibrium. As on the previous page, take any subgame,
        starting at a node <m>m</m> at the beginning of round <m>k</m>.
        There are two possibilities for what happened before <m>m</m>:
        either nobody has gone straight yet, or at least one teenager has
        gone straight at some point.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="activity-chicken-spne-case1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose nobody has gone straight before <m>m</m>. What does
          <m>\gamma</m> tell both teenagers to do at <m>m</m>? Is the
          restriction of <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> to this subgame a Nash
          equilibrium when <m>\delta \geq \frac{1}{2}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\gamma</m> tells both teenagers to swerve, exactly as at the
          start of the game. So the subgame looks just like the whole game,
          with every payoff multiplied by <m>\delta^k</m>, and by <xref ref="activity-chicken-nash"/>,
          the restriction is a Nash equilibrium when
          <m>\delta \geq \frac{1}{2}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="activity-chicken-spne-case2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now suppose someone has gone straight before <m>m</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              What does <m>\gamma</m> tell both teenagers to do in every round
              of the subgame?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              What payoff does each teenager get per round?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If Teenager 2 does what <m>\gamma</m> says, is going straight
              every round Teenager 1's best response?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Is the restriction of <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> to this subgame a
              Nash equilibrium?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          For the first two questions:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Since someone has already gone straight, <m>\gamma</m> tells
              both teenagers to go straight in every round of the subgame.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              They crash every round, so each gets <m>-2</m> per round.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          For the third question, look back at the Chicken table at the top of
          this page: if Teenager 2 goes straight, what does Teenager 1 get by
          swerving instead?
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Since someone has already gone straight, <m>\gamma</m> tells
              both teenagers to go straight in every round of the subgame.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              They crash every round, so each gets <m>-2</m> per round.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              No. If Teenager 2 goes straight every round, Teenager 1 gets
              <m>-1</m> per round by swerving, which is better than <m>-2</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              No. Going straight every round isn't a best response, so the
              restriction of <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> is not a Nash
              equilibrium of this subgame.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="activity-chicken-spne-conclude">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium? Is
          the threat believable?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Look back at the previous part, where we found subgames in which
          the restriction of <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> is not a Nash
          equilibrium.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          No, <m>(\gamma, \gamma)</m> is not subgame perfect, because its
          restriction to any subgame after someone has gone straight is not a
          Nash equilibrium. So the threat is not believable.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p>
    Let's reflect on why the trigger strategy was a subgame perfect Nash
    equilibrium in the Wine Merchant game, but <m>\gamma</m> is not in
    Chicken. The difference is what the players do forever after someone
    breaks the plan. The Wine Merchant game is a version of the Prisoner's
    Dilemma, where your best response to someone defecting is to defect
    too. So carrying out the threat is in your own interest. In Chicken,
    your best response to someone going straight is to swerve, to avoid a
    crash. So the threat to keep crashing forever is not one a rational
    player would actually carry out.
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-6-3" iframe="question-form.html?section=6.3" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
